Context: notes on Teichmüller groupoids and the strata of the stable-curve moduli stacks indexed by combinatorial types G (modular graphs). In my reading, a type G consists of a set S of components (vertices), a genus function g: S -> N, a set of half-edges / branch points A = ⊔_s A_s (the arrow-hat A), and an involution σ on part of A that encodes which branches are glued. The complementary part I is the set of marked points (legs). This page, item d), sets up the 'morphisms of types' G' -> G and their geometric meaning.

The data of the morphism:
- a map u_0: S' -> S with g' = g∘u_0 (the commutative triangle S' -> S -> N), so genus is preserved vertex by vertex;
- for each s' an injection A'_{s'} -> A_{u_0(s')};
- an involution σ' on A' compatible with σ.

Given a curve X of type G over a field k, with normalization X̃ = ⊔_s X̃_s and the k-points (Â_s)_k ⊂ X̃_s, X is obtained from X̃ by identifying points via σ. One builds X' of type G' as follows:
- set X̃'_{s'} := X̃_{u_0(s')}, so a component may be repeated or omitted;
- mark on it the points (Â'_{s'})_k obtained through the injection (Â'_{s'})_k -> (Â_s)_k ⊂ X̃_s;
- glue via σ'.

Since σ' is compatible with σ, the maps X̃'_{s'} = X̃_{u_0(s')} -> X̃ descend to X' -> X. This map is:
- finite and unramified ('fini net');
- an isomorphism on each normalized component;
- sends the marked points I' into I ⊔ Sing X. A leg of G' may map to a half-edge of G, i.e. a node of X: one branch of a node is 'unglued'.

The last lines begin the converse: every finite unramified morphism X' -> X between curves of these types that is componentwise an isomorphism on normalizations, and sends I' into I ⊔ Sing X, should arise from a unique such morphism of types. In modern terms, this identifies 'local-injection / immersion' morphisms of stable (modular) graphs with finite unramified, normalization-preserving maps of nodal curves. Such morphisms include partial normalizations at nodes and the inclusion of subcurves. The page stops ('----') before the converse is proved.
